1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,919
I want to give you one idea in this lecture.

2
00:00:02,919 --> 00:00:06,013
If you take nothing else from this course, take this.

3
00:00:06,013 --> 00:00:10,566
It explains almost every argument you're going to have with engineering, every

4
00:00:10,566 --> 00:00:14,011
feature that slips, and every launch that gets rolled back.

5
00:00:14,011 --> 00:00:14,653
Here it is.

6
00:00:14,653 --> 00:00:18,331
The software you've shipped your whole career is deterministic.

7
00:00:18,331 --> 00:00:19,732
Same input, same output.

8
00:00:19,732 --> 00:00:20,374
Every time.

9
00:00:20,374 --> 00:00:24,344
You learned to manage that kind of software, and you got good at it.

10
00:00:24,344 --> 00:00:28,313
Then you were handed something that does not behave that way at all.

11
00:00:28,313 --> 00:00:30,765
And nobody told you the rules had changed.

12
00:00:31,315 --> 00:00:33,517
Here are the two pictures side by side.

13
00:00:33,517 --> 00:00:38,034
I want you to hold this image, because almost everything else in the course is a

14
00:00:38,034 --> 00:00:39,051
consequence of it.

15
00:00:39,051 --> 00:00:42,382
On the left, the software you've shipped your whole career.

16
00:00:42,382 --> 00:00:46,843
Same input goes in, one output comes out, and it's the same output every single

17
00:00:46,843 --> 00:00:47,125
time.

18
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:50,400
That single dot is why you could test it once and move on.

19
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:51,981
On the right, an AI feature.

20
00:00:51,981 --> 00:00:56,046
Same input goes in, and what comes out is one of those scattered points.

21
00:00:56,046 --> 00:00:58,361
Run it again and you get a different one.

22
00:00:58,361 --> 00:01:00,676
Nothing is broken on the right hand side.

23
00:01:00,676 --> 00:01:03,499
That scatter *is* the product working as designed.

24
00:01:03,499 --> 00:01:07,564
Notice what that does to the word "it" when someone says "does it work".

25
00:01:07,564 --> 00:01:09,089
On the left, "it" is a dot.

26
00:01:09,089 --> 00:01:13,549
On the right, "it" is a cloud, and you happen to be looking at one point in it.

27
00:01:14,099 --> 00:01:18,537
Let me make the distinction precise, because the whole course depends on it.

28
00:01:18,537 --> 00:01:23,324
A deterministic function gives you the same output for a given input, every single

29
00:01:23,324 --> 00:01:23,616
time.

30
00:01:23,616 --> 00:01:25,192
That's the checkout button.

31
00:01:25,192 --> 00:01:28,870
Click it a thousand times, you get a thousand identical orders.

32
00:01:28,870 --> 00:01:31,556
An AI feature gives you a sample from a range.

33
00:01:31,556 --> 00:01:33,424
The range is the actual product.

34
00:01:33,424 --> 00:01:37,394
Any one answer you happen to be looking at is a single draw from it.

35
00:01:37,394 --> 00:01:40,022
Here's how to feel that in about two minutes.

36
00:01:40,022 --> 00:01:44,692
Take a prompt you already use, run it on the same input ten times, and count how

37
00:01:44,692 --> 00:01:48,546
many of those outputs you would genuinely have been happy to ship.

38
00:01:48,546 --> 00:01:50,297
That fraction is your feature.

39
00:01:50,297 --> 00:01:52,458
Not the best one you saw in the demo.

40
00:01:52,458 --> 00:01:53,217
The fraction.

41
00:01:53,217 --> 00:01:56,077
Most people are surprised, and not in a good way.

42
00:01:56,627 --> 00:01:59,844
Look at what that does to the way you manage the thing.

43
00:01:59,844 --> 00:02:03,997
On the left, one test proves it works, because behaviour is repeatable.

44
00:02:03,997 --> 00:02:07,681
A bad result is a defect: someone fixes it, and it stays fixed.

45
00:02:07,681 --> 00:02:10,606
And the question "does it work" has a real answer.

46
00:02:10,606 --> 00:02:12,536
On the right, none of that holds.

47
00:02:12,536 --> 00:02:15,869
One test proves almost nothing, because you saw one draw.

48
00:02:15,869 --> 00:02:20,782
A bad result usually isn't a defect at all, it's the tail of the range doing exactly

49
00:02:20,782 --> 00:02:21,542
what it does.

50
00:02:21,542 --> 00:02:25,578
And "does it work" stops being a question anyone can answer honestly.

51
00:02:25,578 --> 00:02:27,040
That's the whole problem.

52
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:31,777
You're being asked a question that no longer has an answer, using tools built for

53
00:02:31,777 --> 00:02:33,123
the column on the left.

54
00:02:33,673 --> 00:02:35,890
Watch how predictably this plays out.

55
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:37,208
Someone builds a demo.

56
00:02:37,208 --> 00:02:42,061
It's genuinely impressive, because the happy path for these systems is very good.

57
00:02:42,061 --> 00:02:45,896
Leadership sees it, and the feature gets committed to a quarter.

58
00:02:45,896 --> 00:02:50,509
Then it reaches QA, and QA does what QA has always correctly done: tries some

59
00:02:50,509 --> 00:02:53,086
examples, sees sensible answers, passes it.

60
00:02:53,086 --> 00:02:56,621
I want to be clear that this is not a failure of diligence.

61
00:02:56,621 --> 00:03:00,695
Sampling a few cases is the right method for deterministic software.

62
00:03:00,695 --> 00:03:04,950
It's standard practice, applied correctly, to the wrong kind of system.

63
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:05,788
Then it ships.

64
00:03:05,788 --> 00:03:10,522
Real people arrive with inputs nobody thought of, at a volume nobody simulated.

65
00:03:10,522 --> 00:03:11,960
Now the tail is visible.

66
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:12,559
Not often.

67
00:03:12,559 --> 00:03:16,753
Just often enough that support notices, and then the internet notices.

68
00:03:16,753 --> 00:03:19,629
And here's the part that should bother you most.

69
00:03:19,629 --> 00:03:24,423
When the team sits down to argue about whether it's ready, nobody can settle it.

70
00:03:24,423 --> 00:03:29,156
One person saw three great outputs, another saw two terrible ones, and both are

71
00:03:29,156 --> 00:03:30,235
telling the truth.

72
00:03:30,235 --> 00:03:33,650
There's no shared instrument, so the loudest person wins.

73
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:36,833
The root cause is your acceptance criteria.

74
00:03:36,833 --> 00:03:41,487
You've been writing them the same way for years, and the format is sound for

75
00:03:41,487 --> 00:03:42,896
deterministic software.

76
00:03:42,896 --> 00:03:44,365
The summary is accurate.

77
00:03:44,365 --> 00:03:46,202
The classification is correct.

78
00:03:46,202 --> 00:03:48,101
The user gets a helpful answer.

79
00:03:48,101 --> 00:03:52,510
Read those again and ask one specific question: accurate on which input?

80
00:03:52,510 --> 00:03:57,531
Because the honest answer is that it's accurate on some inputs and not others, and

81
00:03:57,531 --> 00:04:02,246
the criterion doesn't say which, or how many, or which failures you'd accept.

82
00:04:02,246 --> 00:04:03,594
So it can't be tested.

83
00:04:03,594 --> 00:04:05,308
It can only be argued about.

84
00:04:05,308 --> 00:04:08,370
A criterion you cannot measure is not a criterion.

85
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:12,730
So here's the replacement, and it's the spine of this whole course.

86
00:04:12,730 --> 00:04:14,437
You pick a set of real inputs.

87
00:04:14,437 --> 00:04:18,418
Fifty is usually enough, and I'll show you exactly how to choose them.

88
00:04:18,418 --> 00:04:23,081
You decide, in advance and in writing, what a good output looks like for each one.

89
00:04:23,081 --> 00:04:24,958
That set becomes your instrument.

90
00:04:24,958 --> 00:04:28,769
Then your acceptance criterion becomes a number on that instrument.

91
00:04:28,769 --> 00:04:32,750
Not "accurate", but ninety-two percent pass rate on those fifty cases.

92
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:37,470
Not "doesn't make things up", but zero unsupported claims on the twenty cases where

93
00:04:37,470 --> 00:04:39,347
you specifically check citations.

94
00:04:39,347 --> 00:04:41,224
Now look at what you just gained.

95
00:04:41,224 --> 00:04:42,702
The criterion is testable.

96
00:04:42,702 --> 00:04:46,172
It re-runs automatically every time someone changes a prompt.

97
00:04:46,172 --> 00:04:49,527
Two people who disagree can go and look at the same number.

98
00:04:49,527 --> 00:04:54,191
And when someone asks whether it's ready, that question has an answer instead of a

99
00:04:54,191 --> 00:04:54,589
debate.

100
00:04:55,139 --> 00:04:59,786
I want to be straight with you about the cost, because most courses skip this bit.

101
00:04:59,786 --> 00:05:01,317
This is more work up front.

102
00:05:01,317 --> 00:05:05,794
Curating fifty cases and agreeing what good looks like takes real hours, and it

103
00:05:05,794 --> 00:05:08,855
happens early, when everyone would rather be building.

104
00:05:08,855 --> 00:05:13,106
You will feel that friction, and someone on your team will push back on it.

105
00:05:13,106 --> 00:05:14,807
Here's the argument that wins.

106
00:05:14,807 --> 00:05:16,110
That work is not extra.

107
00:05:16,110 --> 00:05:19,624
It's the same work you were always going to do, moved earlier.

108
00:05:19,624 --> 00:05:22,402
You were going to argue about quality eventually.

109
00:05:22,402 --> 00:05:27,106
The only question is whether you argue with evidence or with anecdotes, and whether

110
00:05:27,106 --> 00:05:29,657
it happens before launch or after a rollback.

111
00:05:29,657 --> 00:05:33,511
The teams that ship AI features reliably are not smarter than yours.

112
00:05:33,511 --> 00:05:35,325
They built the instrument first.

113
00:05:35,875 --> 00:05:40,576
This is what an acceptance criterion actually looks like once you stop pretending

114
00:05:40,576 --> 00:05:42,085
the feature is a function.

115
00:05:42,085 --> 00:05:45,277
Fifty real inputs, each output scored against a rubric.

116
00:05:45,277 --> 00:05:47,889
The bars show how the quality is distributed.

117
00:05:47,889 --> 00:05:52,648
And your criterion is the line: where you put it, and how much of the distribution

118
00:05:52,648 --> 00:05:54,389
has to sit to the right of it.

119
00:05:54,389 --> 00:05:58,800
In this picture, thirty-four of fifty outputs are at or above the pass mark.

120
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:03,501
That's a sixty-eight percent pass rate, and for most features that's nowhere near

121
00:06:03,501 --> 00:06:04,081
shippable.

122
00:06:04,081 --> 00:06:07,563
But look at what you can now do that you couldn't do before.

123
00:06:07,563 --> 00:06:09,885
You can say exactly how far off you are.

124
00:06:09,885 --> 00:06:13,599
You can re-run this after a prompt change and see the bars move.

125
00:06:13,599 --> 00:06:18,416
You can argue about where the line belongs, which is a productive argument, instead

126
00:06:18,416 --> 00:06:21,667
of arguing about whether it "feels ready", which is not.

127
00:06:21,667 --> 00:06:25,033
The grey bars on the left are also doing something useful.

128
00:06:25,033 --> 00:06:26,832
They aren't a mystery any more.

129
00:06:26,832 --> 00:06:29,966
They're a list of specific inputs you can go and read.

130
00:06:30,516 --> 00:06:33,194
Everything after this builds on that one shift.

131
00:06:33,194 --> 00:06:35,188
In Section 3 you'll write the spec.

132
00:06:35,188 --> 00:06:39,575
In Section 4, the heart of this course, you'll build the measuring instrument

133
00:06:39,575 --> 00:06:44,133
yourself: first in a spreadsheet so you can see every moving part, then in about

134
00:06:44,133 --> 00:06:45,329
thirty lines of code.

135
00:06:45,329 --> 00:06:49,887
Section 7 puts a currency number on the range, because the spread has a cost and

136
00:06:49,887 --> 00:06:52,109
someone is going to ask you what it is.

137
00:06:52,109 --> 00:06:56,610
Section 8 deals with the tail that stops being an engineering bug and becomes a

138
00:06:56,610 --> 00:06:57,692
legal conversation.

139
00:06:57,692 --> 00:06:59,230
But it all comes back here.

140
00:06:59,230 --> 00:07:00,085
Not a function.

141
00:07:00,085 --> 00:07:00,940
A distribution.

142
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:04,643
Next lecture, you're going to feel this rather than just hear it.

143
00:07:04,643 --> 00:07:08,118
I'm going to hand you a real AI output and have you judge it.

144
00:07:08,118 --> 00:07:12,790
It takes five minutes, and what you find out about your own judgement is genuinely

145
00:07:12,790 --> 00:07:13,588
uncomfortable.

146
00:07:13,588 --> 00:07:14,670
I'll see you there.
